numpy 2.4.6  python 3.11.15  mpmath 1.4.1  TZ=IST-5:30
---- datetime64: unit inference and values in every unit
ok   datetime64(str): unit 'automatically selected from the form of the string' (Y, M, D, h, m, s; 1-3 fraction digits ms, 4-6 us, 7-9 ns)
   7 timestamps x 9 units, mismatches: []
ok   astype to Y/M/W/D/h/m/s/ms/us gives floor((t - 1970-01-01)/unit) (truncation toward -inf before the epoch)
   datetime64('2020-01-02','W') = 2020-01-02 (int 2609); days 18263 // 7 = 2609; 1970-01-01 is a Thursday
ok   week unit: value = days // 7 since 1970-01-01, so weeks start on Thursday (2020-01-02 is a Thursday -> itself)
ok   datetime64('2020-03-04T05:06', 'D') truncates to the day; ('2020-03-04', 'Y') to the year
ok   documented examples: datetime64(1,'Y') == '1971'; datetime64('2005-02','D') == '2005-02-01'; '2005' == '2005-01-01'; '2010-03-14T15' == '2010-03-14T15:00:00.00'
ok   generic-unit array from strings picks the finest unit present ('datetime64[ms]' in the documented example)
ok   'NAT' in any case parses to NaT; 'now'/'today' give s / D units
ok   invalid dates raise ValueError: month 13, Feb 30, 1900-02-29, leap second 23:59:60 (documented shortcoming)
   datetime64('2020-03-04T05:06:07.1234567890') -> unit ps, stored -3121623215564649976, exact count 1583298367123456789000, prints '1969-11-25T20:52:56.784435350024'
   datetime64('1970-01-01T00:00:01.1234567890') -> unit ps, stored 1123456789000, exact count 1123456789000, prints '1970-01-01T00:00:01.123456789000'
   datetime64('2020-03-04T05:06:07.1234567890123') -> unit fs, stored -4123467107735740596, exact count 1583298367123456789012300, prints '1969-12-31T22:51:16.532892264259404'
   datetime64('2020-03-04T05:06:07.1234567890123456') -> unit as, stored 8603564775199011584, exact count 1583298367123456789012345600, prints '1970-01-01T00:00:08.603564775199011584'
ok   10 fraction digits -> 'ps' unit; a 1970-01-01 timestamp in ps is stored exactly
FAIL a 2020 timestamp written with >= 10 fraction digits (ps/fs/as, documented span [1969, 1970]) is stored exactly or raises, never a silently different date
   2263-01-01 [ns] -> 1678-06-12T00:25:26.290448384  (exact: False)
   2263-01-01 astype [ns] -> 1678-06-12T00:25:26.290448384  (exact: False)
   1677-01-01 [ns] -> 2261-07-22T23:34:33.709551616  (exact: False)
   1677-01-01 astype [ns] -> 2261-07-22T23:34:33.709551616  (exact: False)
   1971-01-01 [ps] -> 1969-10-30T23:48:31.852580896768  (exact: False)
   1971-01-01 astype [ps] -> EXC OverflowError  (exact: True)
FAIL dates outside the documented ns span [1678, 2262] (and ps span) are rejected or exact, not wrapped (constructor and astype)
   max 's' datetime 292277026596-12-04T15:30:07 (int 9223372036854775807 = 2**63-1: True); one second later -> NaT
ok   '292277026596-12-04T15:30:07' is the largest 's' datetime (2**63-1 s after the epoch, exact)
FAIL one second past the 's' range raises instead of silently becoming NaT (2**63 wraps to the NaT sentinel -2**63)  (got np.datetime64('NaT','s'))
   datetime64(1000000000000000000, 'Y') prints '1000000000000001970'; year 1970+v = 1000000000000001970
   datetime64(9223372036854773837, 'Y') prints '9223372036854775807'; year 1970+v = 9223372036854775807
   datetime64(9223372036854775807, 'Y') prints '-9223372036854773839'; year 1970+v = 9223372036854777777
ok   datetime64(v, 'Y') prints the year 1970 + v for v up to 2**63-1-1970
FAIL datetime64(2**63-1, 'Y') (year 1970+2**63-1 > int64 max, inside the documented 'Y' span of +-9.2e18 years) prints its year
---- datetime64: calendar against Python (leap years 1600-2400, ordinals, negative years)
ok   years 1600..2400: days in year = 365 + calendar.isleap(y) and 'YYYY-02-29' parses iff leap  []
ok   3000 random dates in 1..9999: int value == date.toordinal() - 719163 and str == isoformat
ok   the same 3000 dates: .tolist() returns the datetime.date objects
ok   astronomical years (0 = 1 BC, leap; -4 leap; -100 not leap) and years > 9999: value == proleptic Gregorian days_from_civil  []
   str() of negative / large years: '-0001-03-01'->'-001-03-01', '-0004-02-29'->'-004-02-29', '-0100-02-28'->'-100-02-28', '-9999-01-01'->'-9999-01-01', '0000-01-01'->'0000-01-01', '10000-01-01'->'10000-01-01'
FAIL str() of a datetime64 with a negative year keeps 4 year digits after the sign (ISO 8601 expanded form, like the input '-0001')
ok   str() output of negative years parses back to the same date (round trip)
ok   documented: 1600-01-01 - 0000-01-01 = 50491123200000000 us (584388 days x 86400 s)
ok   documented: (2021-01-01 12:56:23.423 - 2001-01-01)/1 s = 631198583.423 (no leap seconds)
---- datetime64: arithmetic, resolution promotion, Y/M units
ok   timedelta64 addition promotes to the finer unit and the value is exact (Y+M -> M, W+D -> D, D+s -> s, ...)
ok   datetime64[D] - datetime64[s] -> timedelta64[s], exact (2020 leap: 1.5 days = 129600 s)
ok   documented: datetime64('2009') + 20 D = '2009-01-21' (result in D)
ok   documented: 1 W / 1 D = 7.0 and 1 W % 10 D = 7 D
ok   promote_types: m8[Y]+m8[M] -> m8[M]; M8[D]+M8[W] -> M8[D]; m8[7D]+m8[3D] -> m8[D] (gcd of multiples)
ok   Y/M are nonlinear: m8[Y] + m8[D] and m8[M] + m8[D] raise TypeError (no common divisor)
ok   documented: np.timedelta64(timedelta64(1,'Y'), 'D') raises TypeError 'same_kind'; timedelta64(1Y, 'M') = 12 M
   astype('m8[D]'): Y {1: 365, -1: -366, 4: 1460, 100: 36524, 400: 146097, 7: 2556}  truth floor(n*146097/400) {1: 365, -1: -366, 4: 1460, 100: 36524, 400: 146097, 7: 2556};  M {1: 30, -1: -31, 12: 365, 4800: 146097, 5: 152}  truth floor(n*146097/4800) {1: 30, -1: -31, 12: 365, 4800: 146097, 5: 152}
ok   unsafe Y/M -> D uses 'averaged values from the 400 year leap-year cycle' (146097/400 and /4800 days), floored
ok   month arithmetic: datetime64[D] + timedelta64[M] raises (no implicit M->D); M8[M] '2020-01' + 1 M = '2020-02'; Y + M -> M
ok   M8[M] + k months for 1990..2030, k in {1, 13, -1, -25} equals (12y + m - 1 + k) divmod 12
ok   M8[M] -> M8[D] gives the 1st of the month; '2020-01-31' -> M -> +1 M -> D = '2020-02-01' (no end-of-month clipping)
   m8[D] [-1,-7,-8,6,7] -> W [-1, -1, -2, 0, 1];  m8[s] [-1,-3599,-3600,-3601,3599] -> h [-1, -1, -1, -2, 0]
ok   timedelta64 unit conversion floors toward -inf like datetime64 (-1 D -> -1 W, -3601 s -> -2 h)
---- datetime64: NaT
ok   NaT != NaT is True, NaT == NaT False, NaT < x and x < NaT False (datetime and timedelta)
ok   documented: NaT - datetime = NaT timedelta; datetime + NaT timedelta = NaT
ok   np.sort puts NaT at the end (1.18 release note) and argsort agrees
ok   min/max and np.maximum propagate NaT like NaN; np.isnat marks it
ok   timedelta: x / NaT = nan; NaT * 2 = NaT
---- datetime_as_string
ok   documented example: arange 4 steps of 60 m and timezone='UTC' appends 'Z'
ok   documented: unit='h' -> '2002-10-27T04'; unit='s' -> '...:00'; unit='h', casting='safe' raises TypeError
   timezone='local' (TZ=IST-5:30): ['2002-10-27T10:00+0530', '2002-10-27T11:00+0530', '2002-10-27T12:00+0530', '2002-10-27T13:00+0530']; Python astimezone: ['2002-10-27T10:00+0530', '2002-10-27T11:00+0530', '2002-10-27T12:00+0530', '2002-10-27T13:00+0530']
ok   timezone='local': 'convert to the local timezone first, and suffix with a +-#### offset' (fixed +0530)
   (tzdata here has no 'US/Eastern' link; using the identical zone 'America/New_York')
   ZoneInfo('US/Eastern'): ['2002-10-27T00:30-0400', '2002-10-27T01:30-0400', '2002-10-27T01:30-0500', '2002-10-27T02:30-0500']
ok   documented: tzinfo ZoneInfo('US/Eastern') crosses the DST boundary (-0400 -> -0500)
ok   datetime_as_string(unit='D') of pre-epoch instants floors to the calendar day
ok   datetime_as_string default unit shows the stored precision; 'auto' drops trailing zero groups
ok   datetime_as_string(NaT) == 'NaT'
   parsing '2020-01-01T00:00+0100' -> 2019-12-31T23:00, warnings ['UserWarning']
ok   timezone offsets in strings are still converted to UTC (deprecated since 1.11: warns)
ok   ... the warning is a UserWarning (DeprecationWarning before 2.0; 2.0 release note gh-24193: 'now issues a UserWarning')  (got ['UserWarning'])
   note: arrays.datetime.rst still says 'deprecated:: 1.11.0 ... will raise an error in the future'
---- business days against a plain-Python calendar
ok   is_busday == plain-Python (weekday valid in weekmask and not a holiday) for 6 weekmasks x 300 dates with 25 random holidays
ok   busday_count == valid days in [begin, end); when end < begin, minus the valid days in (end, begin] ('not including the day of enddates'), 6 weekmasks x 300 pairs
ok   busday_offset: roll first (nat / forward / following / backward / preceding / modifiedfollowing / modifiedpreceding), then count offset valid days  []
ok   busday_offset(roll='raise') on a weekend raises ValueError (documented 'Non-business day date')
ok   documented examples: '2011-10' 0 fwd -> 2011-10-03; '2012-03' -1 fwd -> 2012-02-29; 3rd Wed Jan 2011 -> 2011-01-19; Mother's day 2012 -> 2012-05-13
ok   documented: busday_count('2011','2012') = 260, weekmask='Sat' -> 53; 2011-07-11..18 = 5 and reversed = -5
ok   weekmask spellings '1111100', [1,1,1,1,1,0,0], bools, 'Mon Tue Wed Thu Fri', 'MonTue Wed  Thu\tFri' agree
ok   weekmask abbreviations are case-sensitive ('mon' raises ValueError); a length-6 weekmask raises
ok   holidays: any order, NaT ignored (documented)
   busdaycalendar normalised holidays: [datetime.date(2011, 7, 1), datetime.date(2011, 7, 4)]  weekmask [True, True, True, True, True, False, False]
ok   busdaycalendar: holidays normalised (sorted, unique, NaT and weekend dates dropped); busdaycal= equals weekmask/holidays
ok   busdaycal together with weekmask raises ValueError ('neither weekmask nor holidays may be provided')
ok   all-zero weekmask raises ValueError (no valid days)
   NaT inputs: is_busday -> False, busday_offset -> ValueError, busday_count -> ValueError  (undocumented)
---- datetime_data, timedelta division / floor division / remainder
ok   datetime_data: M8[25s] -> ('s', 25); m8[D] -> ('D', 1); M8 -> ('generic', 1)
ok   m8[25s] + m8[s] -> m8[s], values 26 and 51 s
ok   timedelta // timedelta == Python int floor division (negative operands floor toward -inf)
ok   timedelta % timedelta == Python int % (result has the sign of the divisor)
ok   divmod(timedelta, timedelta) == (//, %)
ok   timedelta / timedelta == correctly rounded float of the exact ratio
