---- strings: fixed-width storage rules
ok   np.array(['a  ']) keeps trailing spaces (dtype U3, value 'a  ')
ok   trailing NULs are not part of the value: np.array(['a\x00\x00'])[0] == 'a' (dtype still U3), same for bytes
ok   interior NULs are kept: str_len('a\x00b') = 3, str_len('ab\x00') = 2
ok   np.char.strip('\x00a\x00') = '\x00a' (the trailing NUL is already gone; NUL is not whitespace)
ok   chararray: 'values automatically have whitespace removed from the end when indexed'
ok   np.char.equal / not_equal: 'performed by first stripping whitespace characters from the end'; ndarray == does not strip
ok   comparison of U arrays is by code point, like Python ('B' < 'a' < 'é' < '日'); 'ab' < 'abc'
ok   assigning a longer string to a U3 array truncates silently to 3 code points
