ok   genfromtxt defaultfmt names unnamed fields ('var_00', 'var_01')
ok   genfromtxt parses '%.18e' text back bit-exactly (0.1, 5e-324)
ok   genfromtxt strips inline comments after data
---- binary I/O: .npy format parsed by hand, save / load
ok   np.save header for 23 dtypes: '\x93NUMPY' v1.0, little-endian uint16 HEADER_LEN, dict literal with sorted keys descr/fortran_order/shape, '\n'-terminated, total divisible by 64  []
ok   np.save data section == the array's contiguous bytes (C order) for every dtype  []
ok   np.load round trip preserves dtype (incl. byte order '>i4', '>f8', longdouble, M8/m8 units, V4), shape and bytes  []
ok   int32 data bytes equal struct.pack('<6i', ...) (independent encoding)
ok   Fortran-ordered input: fortran_order True, data in column-major order, load returns an F-contiguous equal array
ok   non-contiguous slice is saved in C order with fortran_order False
ok   shape (): header shape (), data 8 bytes = prod(shape) * 8 ('shape=() means there is 1 element'), load shape preserved
ok   shape (0,): header shape (0,), data 0 bytes = prod(shape) * 8 ('shape=() means there is 1 element'), load shape preserved
ok   shape (0, 3): header shape (0, 3), data 0 bytes = prod(shape) * 8 ('shape=() means there is 1 element'), load shape preserved
ok   shape (1, 1, 1): header shape (1, 1, 1), data 8 bytes = prod(shape) * 8 ('shape=() means there is 1 element'), load shape preserved
ok   nested structured dtype with a subarray field: descr == dtype.descr and load round-trips values
ok   object array: np.load default allow_pickle=False raises ValueError; allow_pickle=True returns the objects
ok   np.save(object array, allow_pickle=False) raises ValueError
ok   np.load of a hand-written v1.0 file (big-endian '>u2', fortran_order True, shape (2,3)) gives [[1,2,3],[4,5,6]]
ok   a non-latin-1... unicode field name 'é' is latin-1 encodable -> v1.0; '日' needs utf-8 -> format v3.0 (documented)
ok   header > 65535 bytes (3000 fields) switches to format v2.0 with a uint32 HEADER_LEN, still 64-aligned
ok   np.load(mmap_mode='r') returns a read-only memmap with the same values; np.save appends '.npy' to a bare filename
---- savez / savez_compressed / NpzFile
ok   savez: positional arrays named arr_0, arr_1, keywords by name, members '<name>.npy' in a zip
ok   savez stores members uncompressed (ZIP_STORED); savez_compressed uses ZIP_DEFLATED; the member .npy bytes are identical
ok   np.load(.npz) returns a lazy NpzFile: .files lists names without '.npy', 'in', item and .f attribute access load arrays
ok   NpzFile also accepts the member name with the '.npy' suffix
ok   savez with a positional array and keyword 'arr_0' raises ValueError ('Cannot use un-named variables and keyword arr_0')
ok   an object member of an .npz raises ValueError on access with the default allow_pickle=False
ok   accessing a closed NpzFile raises  (AttributeError)
---- tofile / fromfile / frombuffer / fromstring
ok   tofile writes the raw bytes (struct.pack('<4d')), no header
ok   fromfile(dtype) reads them back; count=2 and offset=8 bytes select elements 1..2
ok   tofile(sep=',', format='%.2f') writes text; fromfile(sep=',') reads it
ok   frombuffer(offset=8 bytes, count=2) -> [30, 40]; read-only view of an immutable bytes object
ok   frombuffer of a bytearray is a writeable view sharing memory (writing element 0 changes the bytearray)
ok   frombuffer with a size not a multiple of the itemsize raises ValueError
ok   fromstring text mode: '1, 2,3' with sep=',' -> [1, 2, 3]; sep=' ' splits on any whitespace
ok   fromstring binary mode (sep='') raises ValueError in 2.3+ ('The binary mode of fromstring now errors, use frombuffer')
ok   fromstring('1,2,x', sep=',') raises ValueError in 2.3+ ('error on bad data')
---- printing: array2string / set_printoptions / repr round trips
ok   floatmode='fixed' precision=3 -> '[0.100 0.250 0.333]' (always exactly precision digits)
ok   floatmode='unique' ignores precision: shortest repr of each value
ok   floatmode='maxprec' -> at most precision digits, per element '[0.1   0.25  0.333]'
ok   floatmode='maxprec_equal' -> same digit count for all '[0.100 0.250 0.333]'
ok   suppress_small=True prints 1e-10 as 0. next to 1; default switches to scientific
ok   threshold=5, edgeitems=2 summarises arange(10) as '[0 1 ... 8 9]'
ok   sign='+' and sign=' '
ok   legacy='1.13' reproduces the old sign padding '[ 1.   0.5]' (modern '[1.  0.5]')
   legacy='1.21' structured repr: "array([(1, 2.)], dtype=[('a', '<i4'), ('b', '<f8')])"
ok   legacy='1.21' is accepted (1.22+)
ok   NEP 51 (2.0): repr(np.float64(1.5)) == 'np.float64(1.5)'; legacy='1.25' restores '1.5'
ok   np.printoptions(precision=2, threshold=3) applies inside the block and restores the previous options on exit  (inside: '[0.33 0.67 1.   2.  ]')
ok   set_printoptions(precision=4) -> str(array([pi])) == '[3.1416]'
ok   str of nan/inf array '[ nan  inf -inf]'
ok   repr of an array with more than threshold=1000 elements is summarised with '...' (documented default)
   default precision=8 / floatmode='maxprec': eval(repr(a)) reproduces 5 of 3004 values exactly (repr is not a round trip by default)
ok   with floatmode='unique' (and threshold=sys.maxsize), eval(repr(a)) is bit-exact for all 3004 float64 values ('unique': shortest repr that uniquely identifies)
ok   ... and for a float32 array (repr carries dtype=float32)
ok   str(np.float64(x)) == Python repr(x) (shortest round-trip digits, same exponent style) for every value
ok   str(np.float32(x)) round-trips and uses the minimal number of digits for 2991 random float32 (Dragon4 'unique')  []
ok   format_float_positional(unique=False, precision=p) == exact binary value rounded half-even at p decimals ('unbiased rounding', Decimal truth)  []
ok   format_float_scientific(unique=False, precision=p) == Decimal mantissa rounded half-even to p digits, exponent at least 2 digits  []
ok   format_float_positional trim: 1.0 -> 'k' '1.', '.' '1.', '0' '1.0', '-' '1' (documented modes)
ok   pad_left=4, pad_right=3 -> '   1.5  '; min_digits=4 -> '1.5000'; exp_digits=3 -> '1.e+005'; fractional=False precision=4 -> '123.5'
ok   format_float_positional(2.0**70 + 2**20, precision=0, unique=False) prints the exact integer; unique 1e23 prints '1' followed by 23 zeros
ok   format_float_positional(unique=True, precision=3) rounds the shortest repr: 1/3 -> '0.333', 0.1 -> '0.1'
ok   float16 / float32 inputs use their own shortest digits (np.float32(0.1) -> '0.1', np.float16(0.1) -> '1.e-01' scientific)
---- structured arrays: layout against ctypes, fields, assignment
ok   align=True: field offsets, itemsize and alignment equal the C layout of the same ctypes.Structure (300 random nested/subarray layouts)
ok   default (packed) structured dtypes: offsets are cumulative sums of itemsizes, no padding (300 layouts)
ok   documented example: [('a','i1'),('b','f8')] has itemsize 9 packed, 16 with align=True (b at offset 8)
ok   dict form with explicit offsets/itemsize: padding respected (itemsize 16, y at 8)
ok   dtype equality: same names/formats/order equal; different names, order or byte order not equal
ok   align=True dtype != the packed dtype with the same fields (different offsets/itemsize)
ok   field titles: a field is reachable by name and by title
ok   single-field access returns a view (writing it changes the array)
ok   multi-field index a[['x','z']] (1.16+) is a view keeping the original offsets and itemsize ('padded' view)
ok   recfunctions.repack_fields removes the padding (itemsize 8)
ok   assignment between structured arrays is by field position, not name (1.14+), with casting
ok   assigning a tuple sets fields in order; assigning a scalar sets every field
ok   structured == compares field-wise element by element
ok   nested dtype: a['pt']['x'] and a[1]['pt']['k'] reach the inner fields
ok   subarray field shape: a['v'].shape == (2, 2, 3)
---- numpy.lib.recfunctions
ok   structured_to_unstructured flattens fields and subarrays in order, promoting to a common dtype (f4, f8 -> f8)
ok   structured_to_unstructured(dtype=int)
ok   structured_to_unstructured on a nested dtype flattens depth-first
ok   unstructured_to_structured is the inverse
   append_fields(base, 'w', [7, 8]) (default usemask=True): MaskedArray, w data [7, 8, None]
ok   append_fields default usemask=True returns a MaskedArray; the shorter new field is padded and masked
ok   append_fields(usemask=False) with two fields: plain ndarray, values appended
   merge_arrays(([1,2,3],[10.5,20.5]), usemask=False): [(1, 10.5), (2, 20.5), (3, -1.0)] dtype [('f0', '<i8'), ('f1', '<f8')]
ok   merge_arrays pads the shorter array with the default fill_value -1 ('fill_value: Filling value used to pad missing data on the shorter arrays')
ok   merge_arrays(flatten=True) concatenates the fields
ok   join_by inner: rows for common keys, sorted by key, colliding field 'a' renamed a1 / a2 (r1postfix='1', r2postfix='2')
ok   join_by outer: union of keys sorted, fields of the missing side masked
ok   join_by leftouter: exactly the keys of r1, r2 fields masked where absent
ok   drop_fields / rename_fields
ok   stack_arrays: records concatenated, missing field masked
---- recarray
ok   np.rec.fromarrays: attribute access r.x, r.y; records support r[1].y
ok   a field named like an ndarray attribute ('size'): r.size is the attribute, r['size'] the field (documented precedence)
ok   np.rec.array(list of tuples, formats, names)
ok   view(np.recarray) shares data: assigning r.u writes the base array
ok   a nested field accessed as an attribute is itself a recarray
---- np.ma: reductions against plain Python on the unmasked data
ok   MaskedArray.sum over the unmasked values (axis None/0/1, 60 random masks) == exact Fraction sum
ok   mean == Fraction mean of the unmasked values
ok   var(ddof=1) == unbiased variance of the unmasked values (n = unmasked count per slice)
ok   std == sqrt(population variance) of the unmasked values
ok   np.ma.median == median of the unmasked values (even counts average the two middle values)
ok   np.ma.average(weights=W) == sum(w x)/sum(w) over unmasked positions only
ok   min / max ignore masked values
ok   count(axis) == number of unmasked entries
ok   fully masked slices give the masked constant for sum/mean/median/average/min/max
ok   np.ma.average(returned=True): the weight sum counts only unmasked positions (1+2+2 = 5), average = (1 + 6 + 8)/5 = 3
ok   np.ma.median of even count [1,2,3,4] = 2.5; all-masked -> masked; per-row with different counts
ok   mean of an int32 masked array is float64 (7/3); var over the unmasked ints
ok   prod ignores masked values; cumsum keeps the mask positions and skips masked values in the running total
---- np.ma: mask propagation, domains, fill values
ok   binary ops: result mask = union of the operand masks (+, *, **, comparisons)
ok   documented: ma.log([-1, 0, 1, 2]) masks the out-of-domain -1 and 0 and gives [--, --, 0.0, log 2]
ok   ma.divide masks division by zero; ma.sqrt masks negatives; ma.arccos masks |x| > 1
ok   the np.ma domain functions raise no RuntimeWarning
ok   np.log (plain ufunc) on a MaskedArray: 'entries of the output masked array are masked wherever ... the input fall outside the validity domain'
ok   integer division by zero in np.ma masks the entry
ok   default fill_value table: bool True, int 999999, float 1e20, complex 1e20+0j, object '?', string 'N/A'
ok   structured default fill value is per field ((999999, 1e20))
   int8 masked array: fill_value np.int64(999999), filled() -> [1, 63] (999999 does not fit int8; warnings [])
FAIL int8 masked array with the default fill value: filled() gives 999999 (the documented int default) or refuses, not a wrapped in-range value  (filled() -> [1, 63])
ok   filled() uses fill_value; filled(0); compressed() returns the unmasked values in C order as 1-D
ok   masked_where(cond, a) masks where cond; masked_invalid masks nan and +-inf
ok   masked_equal: masks x == value and sets fill_value to value (documented)
ok   masked_values uses isclose (rtol 1e-5, atol 1e-8): 1.0000001 masked, 1.1 not; fill_value set to value
ok   masked_inside is inclusive of both endpoints; masked_outside masks strictly outside
ok   masked_greater / masked_less_equal
---- np.ma: cov / corrcoef
ok   ma.cov(x, y) with different masks: 'a common mask is allocated', each entry is the covariance over the jointly unmasked pairs
ok   ma.corrcoef(x, y) == Pearson r over the jointly unmasked pairs
ok   ma.cov with allow_masked=False and masked data raises ValueError
