---- np.ma: sort, argmax, unique, concatenate / stack, count
ok   ma.sort puts masked values last (endwith=True default); endwith=False puts them first
ok   argsort: masked entries last, unmasked in increasing order
ok   argmax / argmin ignore masked values (masked max 5.0 at index 3 is skipped)
   all-masked argmax -> 0 (fill with the minimum; undocumented result)
ok   np.ma.unique: 'Masked values are considered the same element (masked)' -> [1, 2, 3, --]
ok   ma.concatenate preserves the masks
ok   np.concatenate on MaskedArrays returns a MaskedArray but 'the input masks are not preserved' (documented; use ma.concatenate)
ok   ma.stack / ma.vstack / ma.hstack / ma.column_stack preserve masks
ok   count() over all axes and count(axis=0)
---- np.ma: mask sharing, hard masks, polyfit
ok   documented slice example: assigning through a slice unmasks in the original (mask is a view): x.mask -> [F,F,F,F,T], x.data -> [1,-1,3,4,5]
ok   masking through a slice also propagates to the original (x[1] becomes masked)
ok   hard mask: assignment to a masked entry silently keeps it masked; after soften_mask() it unmasks (documented example)
ok   harden_mask(): a slice assignment writes only the unmasked entries
ok   assigning nomask to .mask unmasks everything
ok   ma.array(x, copy=True) owns its mask: unmasking the copy leaves x masked
ok   np.ma.polyfit: 'Any masked values in x is propagated in y, and vice-versa' -> equals the exact LS fit on the 10 jointly unmasked points
   2-D y, mask only in column 0: column-1 coefficients [-1.8334109862528003, 10.693577516337529] vs LS on rows kept by the union mask [-1.833410986252801, 10.693577516337532]
ok   np.ma.polyfit with 2-D y: a row masked in any column is dropped for all columns (documented '2D y ... masked rows')
